  "result": "Fixed. The command now prints `27.06`; it was printing `26.09`.\n\n**Root cause:** `to_cents` in `shop/money.py` padded the decimal digits on the wrong side. It used `frac.rjust(2, \"0\")`, so `\"12.5\"` became `\"12.05\"` and was read as 1205 cents instead of 1250. That gave 2 \u00d7 $12.05 = $24.10, plus 8.25% tax = $26.09. The fix pads on the right with `frac.ljust(2, \"0\")`, so `\"5\"` becomes `\"50\"`. I also dropped the `or 0`, which is no longer needed because an empty fraction now pads to `\"00\"`. With the fix, $25.00 plus $2.06 tax = **$27.06**.\n\n**Tests:** pytest isn't installed, so I ran the suite with `python3 -m unittest`. All 7 tests pass: the 6 existing ones, plus a new regression test in `tests/test_money.py` covering `\"12\"`, `\"12.5\"`, `\"12.05\"` and `\"12.34\"`. None of the existing tests used a one-digit decimal, which is why they never caught this.\n\n**A separate bug I didn't fix:** `to_cents` also gets negative amounts wrong. `\"-1.5\"` comes out as \u2212100 + 50 = \u221250 instead of \u2212150. The CLI doesn't hit this with normal input, but tell me if you want it fixed too.",
